<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<h2>How this calculator depreciates</h2>
<p>It uses straight-line depreciation — age divided by expected life — capped at the percentage you pick. Real estimates may adjust for condition, use different life expectancies or leave some items undepreciated, so treat the output as a planning figure, not the carrier’s exact number.</p>
<h2>Life spans that often appear in Pennsylvania estimates</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Common range</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th>Architectural asphalt shingles</th><td>25–30 years</td></tr><tr><th>Modified-bitumen flat roof</th><td>15–20 years</td></tr><tr><th>Natural slate</th><td>75 years or more</td></tr><tr><th>Interior paint</th><td>8–12 years</td></tr><tr><th>Carpet</th><td>8–12 years</td></tr><tr><th>Water heater</th><td>10–15 years</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Ranges vary by product and estimating platform; these are general reference points.</p>
<h2>Getting the holdback released</h2>
<p>Most replacement cost policies release recoverable depreciation after repairs are finished within a stated period. Send final invoices and completion photos, and set a reminder in the <a href="/claim-calculators/claim-deadline-calendar/">claim deadline calendar</a> so the window doesn’t close on you.</p>

<h2>Questions about this calculator</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Should labor be depreciated?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Practices differ by policy, carrier and court decisions. Ask the carrier to explain in writing how labor is treated in your estimate.</p></div></details><details><summary>What happens if I don’t repair?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Under most replacement cost policies you keep the actual cash value payment but may not collect the held-back depreciation.</p></div></details></div>
